WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.060
An exit node, also called an exit relay, is the point where ordinary web traffic leaves the Tor network and travels to a destination site on the public internet.

2
00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:16.840
The exit relay is necessary when the site is not an onion service because normal websites expect a normal internet connection.

3
00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:21.500
From the website's perspective, the request appears to come from the exit relay.

4
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.480
From the user's perspective, the exit relay is the last Tor hop before the destination.

5
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.480
The exit position creates a trust boundary.

6
00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:36.940
The exit relay should not know the user's original network address, but it may be able to observe traffic that is not protected by end-to-end encryption.

7
00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:44.880
If a user visits an old HTTP-only page, an exit relay could potentially see or modify the page content.

8
00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:47.140
That is why HTTPS matters.

9
00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:57.800
HTTPS creates encryption between the browser and the destination website, so the exit relay can see that a connection is going to a site, but not the protected page content or form data.

10
00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:03.600
HTTPS does not hide everything from an exit relay or from the destination.

11
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:12.500
The exit relay may still see connection metadata, such as destination addresses or names exposed by the connection process, depending on how the site is reached.

12
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:18.920
The destination still sees requests, account logins, browser behavior, and submitted content.

13
00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:25.100
HTTPS protects content in transit; it does not make the website forget what the user does there.

14
00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:28.260
Browser warnings are part of this boundary.

15
00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:36.920
If Tor Browser warns that a connection is not private, or that a certificate problem exists, the user should slow down rather than click through automatically.

16
00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:43.180
A warning may indicate a misconfigured site, a captive portal, or an interception attempt.

17
00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:48.080
Tor changes the network path, but it does not make bad certificates trustworthy.

18
00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:54.820
The same caution used in a normal browser still applies, with higher stakes when privacy is the reason for using Tor.

19
00:01:54.920 --> 00:02:01.160
When in doubt, do not submit passwords or private messages through a connection the browser does not trust.

20
00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:05.120
Some websites treat Tor exit traffic differently.

21
00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:12.820
They may show more captchas, block access, or flag logins because exit relays are shared by many people.

22
00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:16.520
This can be frustrating, but it is not always personal.

23
00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:22.780
A site may see many unrelated users arriving from the same exit address and apply anti-abuse controls.

24
00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:30.320
The practical response is patience and context, not attempts to force access where a service does not allow it.

25
00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:34.840
Exit relays also explain why onion services can be useful.

26
00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:41.900
When a site is available as an onion service, the connection stays inside the Tor network instead of leaving through an exit relay.

27
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:48.640
That can remove the exit relay trust boundary for that service and can provide stronger authentication of the onion address.

28
00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:54.340
It does not make the service trustworthy by itself, but it changes the path and the exposure points.

29
00:02:54.620 --> 00:03:03.740
A simple rule helps: treat the exit as the edge of Tor's transport protection, and treat HTTPS as the protection for web content beyond that edge.

30
00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:17.680
When browsing ordinary websites through Tor, prefer HTTPS, pay attention to browser warnings, avoid sending sensitive data to sites that do not protect the connection, and remember that the destination still receives whatever you choose to provide.

31
00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:22.100
Trust is not eliminated; it is moved into clearer boundaries.
